;;; unbound-symbol.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: unbound-symbol   ·   family: language   ·   counted-real

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — unbound-symbol")

(error-card :key "unbound-symbol"
  :name "Unbound symbol"
  :family "language"
  :census-status "counted-real"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/unbound-symbol.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"R7RS calls this referencing an *unbound variable* — evaluating a symbol that
names no binding in the current environment or any enclosing one. In Motoi it is
the single most common error, because it is the failure the reader reaches for
the instant a name is misspelled, out of scope, or simply not part of the
language."

  :trigger-code "(display frobnicate)"
  :observed-output "error: unbound symbol: frobnicate"
  :normalizes-to "unbound-symbol"

  :meaning
"The evaluator tried to look up the value of a symbol and found nothing bound to
it — not in the local frame, not in any enclosing frame, not in the base
environment. Evaluation stops at that symbol; nothing after it runs. The message
always names the exact symbol it could not resolve, which is your fastest clue.

This is a LANGUAGE error: clean, thrown from eval, and it escapes to the run /
eval / REPL boundary, so the census records it. `(stats/error-count-of
\"unbound-symbol\")` is almost always the largest count in a working session —
that is normal. It is the error you make while you are still typing."

  :common-causes (
    "a typo in a verb or variable name — `carr`, `lenght`, `frobnicate`"
    "a name from another Scheme that Motoi does not ship — `fold-left`, `foldl`,
     `printf`, `1+`, `add1` (these are standard-mismatch unbounds; see broken-examples)"
    "a name used before its `define`, or outside the `let`/`lambda` that binds it"
    "a verb from a consumer dialect (card/*, shop/*, sys/*) run in base Motoi,
     where those verbs are not installed"
    "a quoting mistake: meaning to pass the symbol `'x` but writing `x`, so the
     evaluator tries to look x up instead of quoting it")

  :at-the-verb
"There is no verb this comes from — it fires before any verb runs, during the
lookup of the operator or an argument symbol. If `(some-verb x)` reports
`unbound symbol: some-verb`, the VERB name is unresolved (wrong dialect, typo,
not wired). If it reports `unbound symbol: x`, the ARGUMENT symbol is unresolved
(out of scope, undefined, or should have been quoted)."

  :how-to-detect
"You cannot `guard` a symbol into existence, but you CAN check before you leap.
Test membership before you reference a name you are unsure of:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(define (bound-here? name env-probe) (procedure? env-probe))
(display (if (procedure? car) 'car-is-bound 'car-is-missing))"
  :how-to-detect-output "car-is-bound"

  :fix-shape
"Read the named symbol in the message. Then, in order: (1) is it spelled right?
(2) is it a real Motoi name, or a name from R6RS/Racket/another Scheme? Check the
reference and the broken-examples for standard mismatches. (3) is it in scope
here — defined above this point, or bound by the enclosing `let`/`lambda`? (4)
did you mean to QUOTE it? The fix is never to guess a replacement; it is to bind
the name, correct it to the real name, or quote it."

  :lesson
"An unbound symbol is a promise the code made and the environment could not keep.
The evaluator is being maximally honest: it names exactly what it could not find.
The discipline is to trust that name — do not thrash. Ninety percent of unbounds
are a typo or a standard mismatch, and the message hands you the culprit. This is
also why the census counts them: a spike in one particular unbound key across
sessions is a signal that a name people REACH FOR is missing from the language —
a candidate to wire, alias, or teach against."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-count-of \"unbound-symbol\")"
    "(stats/error-counts 10)"
    "(assoc :total-errors (stats/error-summary))")

  :related-broken-examples ("fold-family.slatl" "hash-short-aliases-dropped.slatl")
  :related-cards ("not-a-procedure" "misc-uncategorized"))
